{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1785702263,"nonce":"x2uGFHgZyrC2XogmFJs29eLHejU=","title":"Decided 2026-08-03 (grill; ADR-0066) as the single gate on three deferred candidates.","message":"**The 2026-08-02 benchmark was single-client.** Depot aggregate throughput under concurrent fan-out is unmeasured — e.g. twenty matrix steps on twenty nodes all fetching the same 8 GB checkout from one Depot. That is the one place the feed could still be a real problem, and it is the axis (concurrency) rather than the axis we measured (per-file cost).\n\n- [ ] Extend `crates/scarab-workspace-client/tests/feed_cost.rs` to N concurrent clients against one Depot; report aggregate throughput and per-client latency at N = 1, 4, 16, 32\n- [ ] Identify what saturates first (CPU, disk, network, connection limits) — the single-client run already showed the harness going CPU-bound at 4x concurrency with client and server sharing cores, so isolate them\n- [ ] State the verdict against the three candidates below\n\n**What the result gates:**\n- **Dragonfly** (CNCF, active) — P2P distribution; only worth it if origin bandwidth is genuinely the constraint\n- **An unprivileged per-node Depot cache DaemonSet** — `wsfetch` hits `$NODE_IP` instead of the cluster Service; cache hits at local-disk speed, misses fall through. **Reach for this BEFORE a P2P mesh**: no privilege, no mounts, no new control plane, and it composes with the Run-hashed affinity design rather than replacing it\n- **Kraken** — REJECTED, archived, fails the maintained-library rule (moderate confidence; verify before citing)","files":null}]}